nverse discipline: an objective-to-affordance mapping that selects the *least* capable tool that still affords the required learner action, then checks it against the cohort's real constraints. The software case you write is the document that proves the choice was reasoned, not sold to you.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Map a learning objective to the specific affordance it requires before naming any tool.\n- Select the least costly tool that affords the required action and survives the constraints.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA selection memo opens with a feature list: \"We will use Tool X (a VR platform) because it has avatars, spatial audio, hand tracking, eye tracking, and a scene editor. It is the most advanced option available.\" Nowhere does it say what the learner must be able to do. The memo has led with capability and never named the objective, so there is no way to tell whether any of those features earns its cost. The decision is not \"is Tool X advanced?\" It is \"what affordance does the objective demand, and what is the cheapest tool that provides it within our constraints?\"\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAn **affordance** is what a tool lets a learner *do*, not what features it lists. The selection discipline is to derive the required affordance from the objective, then match tools to affordances, then filter by constraints, in that order.\n\n1. **Objective to affordance.** Translate the verb in the objective into the interaction it requires. \"Practice giving corrective feedback and read the reaction\" requires conditional branching with visible reaction states. That is an affordance, not a product.\n2. **Affordance to candidates.** List every tool that provides the affordance, including the humble ones. Many affordances are met by far cheaper tools than the flashy default.\n3. **Candidates to constraints.** Filter by the cohort's reality: access (do learners have the hardware?), cost (is there a license budget?), time (does the build fit the window?), support, and accessibility.\n\nTwo ideas keep this honest. The **least-capable-sufficient** heuristic says pick the simplest tool that affords the action, because unused capability is cost and complexity with no learning return. And the **constraint filter** is non-negotiable: a tool that fails an access or time constraint is disqualified no matter how well it affords the action.\n\nOrder the selection discipline correctly, the sequence that keeps tool choice goal-first instead of feature-first.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"State the learning objective and the verb the learner must perform\", \"Derive the required affordance from that verb, named independently of any product\", \"List every tool that provides the affordance, including the humble ones\", \"Choose the least capable tool that still affords the action\", \"Apply the constraint filter (access, cost, time, support, accessibility) as a veto\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA thin claim sells features:\n\n> Tool X is the most advanced option, so we will use it.\n\nA reasoned case maps objective to affordance to constraint:\n\n> The objective needs branching dialogue where a choice changes the simulated employee's response, so the required affordance is conditional branching with visible reaction states. A browser-based branching-scenario tool provides that at far lower cost than the VR platform and meets our no-headset, two-week, free-license constraints. The VR platform's eye tracking serves no stated objective here.\n\nThe improvement is that every retained capability now maps to an objective, and the choice is justified against the cohort's real constraints.\n\n### Annotated Before/After: A Tool-Selection Rationale\n\nHere is a software-case selection memo for a module that teaches new managers to give corrective feedback, revised from feature-chasing to goal-first.\n\n**Before.** Memo: \"We will use Tool X (a VR headset platform) because it has avatars, spatial audio, hand tracking, eye tracking, and a scene editor. It is the most advanced option available.\"\n\nThe diagnosis: the memo leads with a feature list and never states the learning goal. It is a tool in search of a problem, and the heavy setup is unjustified.\n\n**After (goal-first mapping).** Learning goal: managers practice delivering a corrective-feedback conversation and reading the other person's reaction in real time.\n\n1. Objective to affordance: the goal needs branching dialogue where a choice changes the simulated employee's response. The required affordance is conditional branching with visible reaction states.\n2. Candidate fit: Tool X provides branching, but so does a browser-based branching-scenario authoring tool (Tool Y) at far lower cost.\n3. Constraint check: cohort of 30 part-time learners, no headsets, 2-week build window, free license required. Tool Y meets all constraints; Tool X fails the access and time constraints.\n\nDecision: select Tool Y. The eye tracking and spatial audio of Tool X are real features but serve no stated objective here.\n\n**Rejected suggestion.** A vendor demo urged adding Tool X \"for immersion.\" Rejected: immersion is not the construct; reading and responding to a reaction is, and Tool Y supports that within the constraints.\n\nThe improvement is that every retained capability now maps to an objective, and the choice is justified against the cohort's real constraints.\n\nRun the corrective-feedback selection memo through the discipline yourself, choosing goal-first at each step.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Selecting a tool for the corrective-feedback module\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"A vendor pitches a VR platform with avatars, spatial audio, hand tracking, and eye tracking as the most advanced option. Where do you start?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Adopt it because it is the most capable tool\",\"next\":\"feature\",\"feedback\":\"Starting from the tool is technology determinism: you cannot tell which features earn their cost without naming the objective first.\"},{\"label\":\"State what the learner must do: deliver feedback and read a reaction that changes with their choice\",\"next\":\"affordance\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: name the objective first, then derive the affordance.\"}]},\"feature\":{\"prompt\":\"Reconsider: what must the manager actually be able to do?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Deliver corrective feedback and read a reaction that changes with the choice\",\"next\":\"affordance\",\"feedback\":\"Yes. That sentence yields the required affordance.\"}],\"feedback\":\"Feature-first reasoning runs backward and never reaches the objective.\"},\"affordance\":{\"prompt\":\"The required affordance is conditional branching with visible reaction states. Which tools afford it?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Only the VR platform\",\"next\":\"onlyvr\",\"feedback\":\"A browser-based branching-scenario tool also affords conditional branching at far lower cost.\"},{\"label\":\"Both the VR platform and a browser branching-scenario tool\",\"next\":\"both\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: the affordance does not by itself select VR.\"}]},\"onlyvr\":{\"prompt\":\"Reconsider: does any cheaper tool also provide conditional branching?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Yes, a browser branching-scenario tool does too\",\"next\":\"both\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Now the constraint filter decides.\"}],\"feedback\":\"Branching is not unique to VR.\"},\"both\":{\"prompt\":\"Cohort: 30 part-time learners, no headsets, 2-week build, free license required. Apply the constraint filter.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Pick VR for immersion\",\"next\":\"immersion\",\"feedback\":\"VR fails the access (no headsets) and time constraints, and immersion is not the construct.\"},{\"label\":\"Pick the browser tool: it affords branching and meets every constraint\",\"next\":\"resolved\",\"feedback\":\"Correct: least capable sufficient tool that survives the veto.\"}]},\"immersion\":{\"prompt\":\"Reconsider: which constraints does VR fail, and is immersion the construct?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"VR fails access and time; reading a reaction is the construct, so pick the browser tool\",\"next\":\"resolved\",\"feedback\":\"Yes. The eye tracking served no objective here.\"}],\"feedback\":\"Immersion is not a construct and cannot justify an affordance.\"},\"resolved\":{\"prompt\":\"Resolved. Objective then affordance then constraint selected the browser tool, with VR vetoed on access and time.\",\"feedback\":\"Every retained capability maps to an objective.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\nSelect in the order objective, affordance, constraint, never feature-first:\n\n1. **State the objective and derive the affordance.** No tool is named until the required learner action is written as an affordance.\n2. **Pick the least capable sufficient tool.** Among tools that afford the action, choose the simplest; extra capability is unjustified cost.\n3. **Apply the constraint filter as a veto.** Access, cost, time, support, and accessibility can disqualify any tool regardless of its affordances.\n\nIf a feature in your chosen tool maps to no objective, it is decoration you are paying to maintain.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is equating \"advanced\" with \"appropriate\"; the most capable tool is rarely the best fit and often fails a constraint. The second is letting a tool optimize the surface of a deliverable while the underlying objective-to-affordance mapping is never written, so no one can say why the tool was chosen. The third is treating \"immersion\" or \"engagement\" as objectives; they are not constructs, and they cannot justify an affordance on their own.\n\n<inline-check question=\"The objective shifts to performing a hands-on equipment calibration with correct spatial positioning, and the site already owns headsets. Does the VR platform now become the right choice?\" explanation=\"The required affordance changed to spatial, embodied manipulation, which a browser tool cannot provide, and the access constraint that vetoed VR (no hardware) no longer holds. The same objective-affordance-constraint procedure now selects VR, because the objective and constraints changed, not because VR is inherently better.\" options='[{\"text\":\"No, the browser tool is always the cheaper and therefore correct choice\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Yes, the affordance now requires spatial manipulation and the hardware constraint no longer vetoes VR\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Yes, because VR is the most advanced option available\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nReverse-engineer the bad memo. Strip away the feature list and ask what the manager must actually be able to do: deliver corrective feedback and read a reaction that changes based on their choice. That sentence yields exactly one required affordance, conditional branching with visible reaction states. Now list every tool that affords it; both the VR platform and a browser scenario tool qualify, so the affordance does not select VR. Apply the constraints, 30 part-time learners, no headsets, two weeks, free license, and VR is vetoed on access and time while the browser tool survives. Notice that eye tracking never entered the reasoning, because no objective demanded it. The walkthrough exposes feature-chasing as reasoning run backward: it starts from the tool and never reaches the objective, so it cannot tell which features are paid-for decoration.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead every tool claim by asking which objective it serves and which constraint might veto it. A strong reader can take a feature list and sort it into affordances the objective requires versus capabilities that serve no goal here. Keep a two-column note: feature on the left, the objective it serves (or \"none\") on the right.\n\nA strong reader of choosing a software case can answer four questions:\n\n1. What is the required affordance, stated independently of any product?\n2. What is the least capable tool that provides it?\n3. Which constraint, if any, vetoes the flashy default?\n4. Which features in the chosen tool map to no objective?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe case is strong when it derives the affordance from the objective, names the least sufficient tool, and shows the constraint filter doing real work (vetoing at least one candidate). \"Tool X is advanced\" is weak; \"objective needs conditional branching; browser tool affords it; VR vetoed on access and time\" is strong. If the memo lists features with no objective mapping, mark the gap and ask BigAL to map each feature to an objective rather than to summarize the tool.\n\nA useful final entry would read: \"I selected the browser scenario tool because it affords conditional branching within our no-headset, two-week constraints. I rejected the VR platform because its extra affordances map to no objective and it fails the access constraint.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nNow imagine the objective shifts to \"perform a hands-on equipment calibration with correct spatial positioning,\" and the cohort works at a site that already owns headsets. The required affordance changes to spatial, embodied manipulation, which a browser scenario tool cannot provide, and the constraint that vetoed VR (no hardware) no longer holds. The same objective-affordance-constraint procedure now selects the VR platform, and that is the correct answer, because the objective and constraints changed, not because VR is \"better.\" A designer who reasoned from affordances can flip the choice with confidence; one who picked by feature count cannot explain why VR was wrong before and right now.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/01-06-choosing-a-software-case-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"01.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **Mayer multimedia learning**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the storyboard frame sequence with narration, signaling, and reader-action notes for AIL 606 01.06. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to cognitive load theory. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_choosing_a_software_case\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure the case derives the affordance from the objective, names the least sufficient tool, shows the constraint filter vetoing at least one candidate, and records any feature or suggestion you rejected for mapping to no objective.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nLink the objective-to-affordance discipline to the evidence-based revision of the software case and the bounded support AI can give in mapping features to objectives.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_choosing_a_software_case\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- Mayer multimedia learning. https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_1_6_choosing_a_software_case_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_choosing_a_software_case", "stem": "Three teams pitch a software case for their design project. Which selection rationale is strongest for a graduate ID case study?", "options": ["We chose the tool with the most features so we have room to grow.", "We chose this tool because its core affordance, branching scenarios, directly supports our learning goal of decision practice, and our learner and time constraints fit its setup cost.", "We chose the newest tool because it is trending.", "We chose the tool our instructor seemed to like."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A defensible case selection ties a specific affordance of the tool to the learning goal and checks fit against learner and resource constraints, rather than counting features.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_1_6_case_more_features_better", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_choosing_a_software_case"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_1_6_choosing_a_software_case_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_choosing_a_software_case", "stem": "A team starts by listing the flashy features of a VR tool, then tries to invent a learning goal that uses them. Why does this sequence weaken the case?", "options": ["Because VR is never appropriate for instruction.", "Because it reverses the design logic: the learning goal and audience should drive tool selection, not the tool's features driving the goal.", "Because flashy features always improve learning outcomes.", "Because teams should choose tools without considering features at all."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Goal-first design selects a tool whose affordances serve a defined goal. Feature-first selection produces solutions in search of a problem.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_1_6_case_tool_drives_goal", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_choosing_a_software_case"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_1_6_choosing_a_software_case_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "ail606_supplement_choosing_a_software_case", "stem": "Two tools can both reach the learning goal. Tool A needs a 2-week build and paid licenses; Tool B needs 3 days and is free for the cohort. The case study still picks Tool A with no stated reason. What is the selection flaw?", "options": ["There is no flaw; the more expensive tool is always more rigorous.", "The case ignores constraint analysis: when goal fit is comparable, cost, time, and access constraints should justify the choice, and an unexamined pick is not defensible.", "Free tools can never meet a learning goal.", "The team should pick whichever tool has more buttons."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "When affordance fit is comparable, the defensible case weighs constraints such as time, cost, and learner access. A choice made without that analysis is not justified.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_1_6_case_ignore_constraints", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_choosing_a_software_case"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_1_6_choosing_a_software_case_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "evidence_based_revision", "stem": "What single piece of evidence best demonstrates that a chosen software case actually fits the instructional problem?", "options": ["A screenshot showing the tool looks modern.", "A short mapping that links each learning objective to the specific tool affordance that supports it, with constraints noted.", "A list of every feature the tool offers.", "A note that the team enjoyed using the tool."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "An objective-to-affordance mapping with constraints makes the fit inspectable, showing the case was selected for the problem rather than for novelty.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_1_6_case_tool_drives_goal", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision"]}]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_1_6_case_more_features_better", "pattern": "feature_count_equals_quality", "description": "Selecting a software case because it has the most features, equating feature count with instructional value.", "trigger": "team justifies a tool by how many features it offers", "feedback": "Select for fit, not feature count. Tie one specific affordance to the learning goal; unused features add complexity, not value.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_1_6_case_tool_drives_goal", "pattern": "tool_first_design", "description": "Starting from a tool's appealing features and inventing a learning goal to fit them, reversing the design logic.", "trigger": "team lists tool features first and derives the goal afterward", "feedback": "Lead with the learning goal and audience, then choose a tool whose affordances serve that goal. Avoid solutions in search of a problem.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_1_6_case_ignore_constraints", "pattern": "ignore_resource_constraints", "description": "Choosing a software case without weighing time, cost, and learner-access constraints when goal fit is comparable.", "trigger": "team picks a tool without any constraint analysis", "feedback": "When affordance fit is comparable, justify the choice with constraints: setup time, cost, and learner access. Record the tradeoff.", "rail_action": "ask"}]}